    "---\n",
    "title: \"Geometry of Embedding Spaces\"\n",
    "subtitle: \"Similarity, dimension reduction, clustering, and high-dimensional caution\"\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
    "Word embeddings turn tokens into points in a vector space.  Once language objects become vectors, NLP inherits the language of **geometry**: distances, angles, projections, subspaces, neighborhoods, clusters, and directions.  This chapter develops the geometry of embedding spaces carefully, with emphasis on the mathematical assumptions behind similarity search and visualization.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Learning goals\n",
    "\n",
    "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Treat a collection of word or sentence embeddings as a point cloud $X \\in R^{n \\times m}$.\n",
    "- Compare inner-product similarity, cosine similarity, and Euclidean distance.\n",
    "- Explain why nearest-neighbor lists depend on normalization and metric choice.\n",
    "- Derive PCA from the singular value decomposition of a centered embedding matrix.\n",
    "- Interpret low-dimensional embedding visualizations responsibly.\n",
    "- State the $k$-means objective and understand clustering as an optimization problem.\n",
    "- Describe anisotropy, hubness, and distance concentration in high-dimensional spaces.\n",
    "- Use linear directions to study interpretable attributes and possible bias in embeddings.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9.1 Why geometry matters in NLP\n",
    "\n",
    "In earlier chapters, a word embedding was introduced as a map\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "E:V \\longrightarrow R^m,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $V$ is a vocabulary and $m$ is the embedding dimension.  For a token $w \\in V$, the vector $E(w)$ is often written as $e_w \\in R^m$.\n",
    "\n",
    "If $V = \\{w_1,\\dots,w_n\\}$, the embedding model produces a matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X=\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
    "- & e_{w_1}^T & -\\\\\n",
    "- & e_{w_2}^T & -\\\\\n",
    "& \\vdots & \\\\\n",
    "- & e_{w_n}^T & -\n",
    "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "\\in R^{n\\times m}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The rows of $X$ form a finite point cloud in $R^m$.  Many NLP operations can then be written geometrically.\n",
    "\n",
    "| NLP question | Geometric formulation |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Which words are similar to `king`? | Find nearest neighbors of $e_{king}$. |\n",
    "| Which documents match a query? | Find vectors with large inner product or cosine similarity to the query vector. |\n",
    "| Are topics separated? | Look for clusters or low-dimensional structure. |\n",
    "| What direction represents an attribute? | Estimate a vector direction $b$ and project embeddings onto $b$. |\n",
    "| Can we visualize embeddings? | Project $R^m$ to $R^2$ using PCA, SVD, or another map. |\n",
    "\n",
    "The important point is that geometry is not merely a visualization tool.  It is part of the model.  A statement such as “these words are close” is incomplete until we specify the representation, the metric, and any preprocessing such as centering or normalization.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.math-card}\n",
    "**Core viewpoint.**  An embedding space is a finite metric or inner-product structure induced by a representation map.  The geometry we observe is not intrinsic to language alone; it depends on the construction of $E$, the training data, the loss function, and the similarity measure.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9.2 Similarity measures\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $x,y \\in R^m$ be two embedding vectors.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Inner product\n",
    "\n",
    "The inner product is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\langle x,y\\rangle = x^T y = \\sum_{j=1}^m x_j y_j.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Large inner products favor vectors that point in similar directions and also have large norms.  Therefore inner-product retrieval is sensitive to vector length.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Euclidean distance\n",
    "\n",
    "The Euclidean distance is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "d_2(x,y)=\\|x-y\\|_2=\n",
    "\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^m (x_j-y_j)^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Small Euclidean distance means that the vectors are close as points in $R^m$.  It uses both direction and length.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Cosine similarity\n",
    "\n",
    "The cosine similarity is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\cos(x,y)=\\frac{x^T y}{\\|x\\|_2\\|y\\|_2},\n",
    "\\qquad x\\ne 0,\\ y\\ne 0.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Cosine similarity depends only on the angle between $x$ and $y$.  It is invariant under positive rescaling:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\cos(cx,dy)=\\cos(x,y),\n",
    "\\qquad c>0,\\ d>0.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is useful when vector length is less meaningful than direction.  In many word and document embedding applications, cosine similarity is preferred for nearest-neighbor search.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Relationship after normalization\n",
    "\n",
    "Define the normalized vectors\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{x}=\\frac{x}{\\|x\\|_2},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\hat{y}=\\frac{y}{\\|y\\|_2}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\|\\hat{x}-\\hat{y}\\|_2^2\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\|\\hat{x}\\|_2^2+\n",
    "\\|\\hat{y}\\|_2^2-2\\hat{x}^T\\hat{y}\n",
    "=2-2\\cos(x,y).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus, for normalized embeddings, minimizing Euclidean distance is equivalent to maximizing cosine similarity.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.math-card}\n",
    "**Metric choice matters.**  The nearest neighbors of a word can change when we switch from inner product to cosine similarity or Euclidean distance.  This is not a numerical accident.  It reflects different mathematical assumptions about whether vector length carries semantic information.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9.3 Nearest neighbors as an optimization problem\n",
    "\n",
    "Given embeddings $e_1,\\dots,e_n \\in R^m$ and a query vector $q \\in R^m$, nearest-neighbor search solves\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{NN}_s(q)\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\arg\\max_{1\\le i\\le n} s(q,e_i),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $s$ is a similarity function such as inner product or cosine similarity.  Equivalently, using a distance $d$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{NN}_d(q)\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\arg\\min_{1\\le i\\le n} d(q,e_i).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For top-$k$ retrieval, we return the $k$ largest scores or the $k$ smallest distances.\n",
    "\n",
    "In matrix form, if $X\\in R^{n\\times m}$ stores the candidate embeddings as rows, inner-product scores are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "s = Xq \\in R^n.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "For cosine similarity, define a row-normalized matrix $\\widehat X$ and normalized query $\\hat q$.  Then\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "s = \\widehat X \\hat q.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This simple matrix-vector multiplication is one reason embedding retrieval is computationally attractive.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9.4 A small geometric example\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose an embedding model gives the following two-dimensional vectors:\n",
    "\n",
    "| token | $x_1$ | $x_2$ |\n",
    "|---|---:|---:|\n",
    "| `cat` | 1.0 | 0.9 |\n",
    "| `dog` | 1.1 | 0.8 |\n",
    "| `lion` | 1.4 | 1.1 |\n",
    "| `car` | -1.0 | -0.8 |\n",
    "| `truck` | -1.2 | -0.9 |\n",
    "| `bus` | -0.9 | -1.1 |\n",
    "| `king` | 0.2 | 1.8 |\n",
    "| `queen` | 0.1 | 1.7 |\n",
    "\n",
    "This toy space has three visible regions: animals, vehicles, and royalty.  But even in this simple example, a nearest-neighbor query depends on whether we use cosine similarity or Euclidean distance.  If two points have similar directions but different magnitudes, cosine and Euclidean rankings can disagree.\n",
    "\n",
    "The next interactive example lets you explore this dependence directly.\n",
    "\n",
