   "source": [
    "The same vectors can produce different neighbor rankings.  In a real embedding model, such differences can affect search results, clustering results, and qualitative interpretations.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9.7 Centering, normalization, and anisotropy\n",
    "\n",
    "Embedding spaces often have strong global directions.  For example, many vectors may share a large common component.  This can make most pairwise cosine similarities positive and reduce the contrast between meaningful neighbors and unrelated words.\n",
    "\n",
    "Let the rows of $X\\in R^{n\\times m}$ be embeddings.  The sample mean vector is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\bar{x}=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The centered embedding matrix is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X_c = X - {\\bf 1}\\bar{x}^T,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where ${\\bf 1}\\in R^n$ is the vector of all ones.  Centering removes the global mean direction.\n",
    "\n",
    "Row normalization gives\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\tilde{x}_i=\\frac{x_i}{\\|x_i\\|_2}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Depending on the task, one may use raw vectors, centered vectors, normalized vectors, or centered-and-normalized vectors.  These choices change the geometry.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.math-card}\n",
    "**Anisotropy.**  A point cloud is anisotropic when its variance is much larger in some directions than in others.  In embedding spaces, anisotropy can cause a few dominant directions to affect many similarity computations.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "A simple diagnostic is to inspect the singular values of the centered matrix.  If the first few singular values dominate, then much of the variation lies in a low-dimensional subspace.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9.8 PCA for embedding spaces\n",
    "\n",
    "PCA is a standard method for projecting high-dimensional embeddings to a lower-dimensional space.  It should be understood as a variance-preserving linear projection, not as a perfect map of semantic meaning.\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $X\\in R^{n\\times m}$ be the embedding matrix, and let\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X_c = X - {\\bf 1}\\bar{x}^T\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "be the centered matrix.  The sample covariance matrix is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "S=\\frac{1}{n-1}X_c^T X_c \\in R^{m\\times m}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "PCA finds orthonormal directions $v_1,\\dots,v_r$ solving\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "v_1 = \\arg\\max_{\\|v\\|_2=1} v^T S v,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and, for $j>1$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "v_j = \\arg\\max_{\\|v\\|_2=1,\\ v\\perp v_1,\\dots,v_{j-1}} v^T S v.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The solutions are eigenvectors of $S$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "S v_j = \\lambda_j v_j,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\lambda_1\\ge \\lambda_2\\ge \\cdots \\ge 0.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Equivalently, use the singular value decomposition\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X_c = U\\Sigma V^T.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The columns of $V$ are the principal directions, and the low-dimensional PCA coordinates are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Z_k = X_c V_k \\in R^{n\\times k},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $V_k=[v_1,\\dots,v_k]$.\n",
    "\n",
    "The fraction of variance explained by the first $k$ components is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\frac{\\lambda_1+\\cdots+\\lambda_k}{\\lambda_1+\\cdots+\\lambda_m}\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\frac{\\sigma_1^2+\\cdots+\\sigma_k^2}{\\sigma_1^2+\\cdots+\\sigma_r^2}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### PCA is useful but limited\n",
    "\n",
    "PCA preserves large-variance linear structure.  It does not necessarily preserve all nearest-neighbor relationships, clusters, or nonlinear geometry.  A two-dimensional PCA plot is a useful diagnostic, but it should not be treated as the embedding space itself.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9.9 Python example: PCA by SVD"
