    "## 9.10 Clustering embeddings\n",
    "\n",
    "Clustering asks whether embedding vectors can be partitioned into groups with similar geometry.  A common model is $k$-means clustering.\n",
    "\n",
    "Given embeddings $x_1,\\dots,x_n\\in R^m$, the $k$-means objective is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\min_{C_1,\\dots,C_k,\\ \\mu_1,\\dots,\\mu_k}\n",
    "\\sum_{r=1}^k \\sum_{i\\in C_r} \\|x_i-\\mu_r\\|_2^2,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $C_1,\\dots,C_k$ partition $\\{1,\n",
    "\\dots,n\\}$ and $\\mu_r\\in R^m$ is the centroid of cluster $r$.\n",
    "\n",
    "For fixed clusters, the optimal centroid is the sample mean:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\mu_r = \\frac{1}{|C_r|}\\sum_{i\\in C_r} x_i.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The standard $k$-means algorithm alternates between two steps:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Assign each point to the nearest centroid.\n",
    "2. Replace each centroid by the mean of the points assigned to it.\n",
    "\n",
    "This decreases the objective at every iteration, but it can converge to a local minimum.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Spherical clustering\n",
    "\n",
    "If cosine similarity is the desired geometry, it is common to normalize vectors before clustering.  Applying $k$-means to normalized vectors is related to spherical clustering, where direction matters more than length.\n",
    "\n",
