    "## 9.14 Semantic directions and analogy geometry\n",
    "\n",
    "Embedding spaces often encode attributes as approximate directions.  For example, the analogy pattern\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "e_{king}-e_{man}+e_{woman}\\approx e_{queen}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "suggests that the difference vector\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "b = e_{woman}-e_{man}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "may represent a gender-related direction in that embedding space.\n",
    "\n",
    "More generally, if we have paired examples $(a_i,b_i)$ representing an attribute change, we can estimate a direction\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "d = \\frac{1}{r}\\sum_{i=1}^r (e_{b_i}-e_{a_i}).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then the scalar projection of a word vector $x$ onto this direction is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{score}_d(x)=\\frac{x^T d}{\\|d\\|_2}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This score measures how far $x$ lies along the direction $d$.  Such directions can be useful for interpretation, but they must be used carefully: a direction learned from a small set of examples may reflect social bias, corpus artifacts, or confounding variables.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.math-card}\n",
    "**Bias as geometry.**  If an embedding space encodes an undesirable association as a direction or subspace, then downstream models that use the embeddings may inherit that association.  Geometric analysis can reveal such structure, but it does not automatically solve the ethical or statistical problem.\n",
