    "## 9.16 Debiasing as projection: a mathematical sketch\n",
    "\n",
    "A simple geometric debiasing operation removes the component of a vector along a direction $b\\ne 0$.\n",
    "\n",
    "The projection of $x$ onto the span of $b$ is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\operatorname{proj}_b(x)=\\frac{x^T b}{b^T b}b.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The component orthogonal to $b$ is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_{\\perp}=x-\\operatorname{proj}_b(x).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This operation guarantees\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_{\\perp}^T b = 0.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "However, this does not guarantee that all bias has been removed.  Bias may live in a higher-dimensional subspace, in nonlinear structure, or in downstream training data.  Projection is a useful mathematical tool, but it is not a complete social or statistical solution.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9.17 Static versus contextual embedding geometry\n",
    "\n",
    "The previous chapter focused mostly on static embeddings, where each word type has one vector:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "E:V\\to R^m.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Modern transformer models produce contextual embeddings.  A token's vector depends on the full input sequence:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "H_t = F_t(w_1,\\dots,w_T) \\in R^m.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus the same word can have different vectors in different contexts.  For example, `bank` in “river bank” and `bank` in “savings bank” may have different contextual embeddings.\n",
    "\n",
    "The geometry of contextual embeddings is therefore a geometry of token occurrences rather than only word types.  The data matrix may contain many rows for the same word in different contexts:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X =\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
    "- & H_{t_1}^{(1)T} & -\\\\\n",
    "- & H_{t_2}^{(2)T} & -\\\\\n",
    "& \\vdots & \\\\\n",
    "- & H_{t_n}^{(n)T} & -\n",
    "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This shift is important.  Static embedding geometry asks: “Where is the word type?”  Contextual embedding geometry asks: “Where is this occurrence of the word in this sentence?”\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9.18 What can go wrong in embedding visualization?\n",
